# Core display names
msgid "人类"
msgstr "Humans"

msgid "偃偶"
msgstr "Yan'ou"

msgid "南柯"
msgstr "Nanke"

msgid "桃花妖"
msgstr "Peach Blossom Spirits"

msgid "比翼"
msgstr "Biyi"

msgid "驻岁"
msgstr "Zhushui"

msgid "医学会"
msgstr "Medical Society"

msgid "听弦塔"
msgstr "Tingxian Towers"

msgid "岁契基金"
msgstr "Long Covenant Fund"

msgid "永乐局"
msgstr "Yongle Bureau"

msgid "蓬莱公所"
msgstr "Penglai Guildhall"

msgid "造身公所"
msgstr "Bodymaking Guild"

msgid "铅字报馆"
msgstr "Lead-Type Press"

# Race descriptions
msgid "蓬莱的人类议员首先是大明的朝使。他们依靠永乐港维持朝贡、海贸和一条需要不断补充未然的全球航路，而蓬莱原生种族越被期限、竞争和劳作压迫，产生的未然也越多。因此朝廷总能把更重的征敛、更低的工钱和更繁忙的贸易解释成“国用所需”，并要求更多议员听从永乐轮运局——不是因为人类更多，而是因为控制航路便足以控制很多事情。"
msgstr "Penglai's human legislators are first and foremost envoys of the Great Ming. They rely on Yongle Harbor to sustain tribute, maritime trade, and a global route that constantly consumes Weiran. The more Penglai's native peoples are pressed by deadlines, competition, and labor, the more Weiran they produce. The court can therefore justify heavier levies, lower wages, and busier trade as necessities of state, while demanding that more legislators heed the Yongle Bureau—not because humans are more numerous, but because controlling the route is enough to control many other things."

msgid "朝使把税课与生产视为维持联合官署、岁贡和远洋航路的根本。征敛不足或产出落后，都会被解释为蓬莱正在脱离朝廷能够掌握的秩序。"
msgstr "The imperial envoys regard taxation and production as the foundations of the joint administration, annual tribute, and ocean routes. Weak revenues or lagging output are read as signs that Penglai is slipping beyond the court's grasp."

msgid "偃偶既是有姓名、有责任、有选票的公民，又是能够买卖、出租、抵押和出口的商品。它们要求更完整的人格权，却并不打算因此放弃身契，反而认为只有真正拥有自己，才有资格决定怎样出售自己。偃偶争取的因此不是逃离市场，而是取得作为一个完整的人进入市场、甚至把自己卖得更彻底的权利。"
msgstr "Yan'ou are citizens with names, duties, and votes, yet also commodities that can be bought, leased, mortgaged, and exported. They demand fuller personhood without intending to abolish body contracts; on the contrary, they argue that only those who truly own themselves have the right to decide how to sell themselves. Their struggle is not to escape the market, but to enter it as complete persons—even to sell themselves more completely."

msgid "偃偶不再以公共指标形成独立的种族年度期望；其议席的普通政治压力来自造身公所。"
msgstr "Yan'ou no longer form an independent annual expectation around public metrics; the ordinary political pressure on their seats comes from the Bodymaking Guild."

msgid "南柯睡着时会进入一个已经实现其理想社会的“理想国”，醒来后却承担着蓬莱最繁重的取能、维修与未然工程。别人嘲笑他们贪睡，他们却比谁都清楚现实本可以有另一种活法。因此南柯要求更好的工钱与生活，并试图用工会、合作社，最终把梦里已经存在的制度真正造进现实。"
msgstr "When Nanke sleep, they enter a Utopia where their ideal society already exists; awake, they shoulder Penglai's hardest energy extraction, maintenance, and Weiran engineering. Others mock them for sleeping too much, but no one knows better that reality could be organized differently. Nanke therefore demand better wages and lives, using unions and cooperatives in an attempt to build into reality the institutions that already exist in their dreams."

msgid "南柯承担蓬莱最重的现实劳动，也最在意劳动是否还有位置、成果是否能换成生活。就业或消费落后时，他们会认为现实正在离梦中那个更好的社会越来越远。"
msgstr "Nanke bear Penglai's heaviest physical labor and care most about whether there is still work to do and whether its fruits can sustain a life. When employment or consumption falls behind, they see reality drifting farther from the better society of their dreams."

msgid "桃花妖曾生活在会主动遮蔽道路的桃源里，土地、宗族与生活资料很少需要服从外界的价格和期限。现代市场却把粮食、木材、土地和劳作都变成税课、货价和不断催促的供货单。于是他们相信疲惫并不是因为世界还不够开放，而是因为道路开得太多了——只要把权力重新收回地方，把往来慢下来，生活自然会恢复秩序。"
msgstr "Peach Blossom Spirits once lived in Taoyuan, whose paths concealed themselves from outsiders. Land, kinship, and the means of life rarely had to obey outside prices or deadlines. Modern markets turned grain, timber, land, and labor into taxes, prices, and ever-urgent delivery orders. They therefore believe exhaustion comes not from a world that is insufficiently open, but from having opened too many roads: return power to the local community, slow exchange down, and life will recover its order."

msgid "桃源关心的不是账面扩张，而是土地与生命资料是否仍然丰足，并真正进入日常生活。生产或消费落后时，他们会把原因归咎于外界征敛、贸易和现代化对地方生活的侵入。"
msgstr "Taoyuan cares less about growth on paper than whether land and the means of life remain abundant and actually reach everyday households. When production or consumption falls behind, they blame outside levies, trade, and modernization for intruding on local life."

msgid "比翼曾替蓬莱保存文字、历法与知识，也因此长期把自己视为本土文化最可靠的解释者。汉语官署、印刷、海贸教育和外来技术让这种地位迅速消失，他们便把失业、传承断裂和机会减少理解成外来者夺走了原本属于自己的位置。于是，比翼越来越希望让本族语言、知识传统乃至政治组织重新成为蓬莱的中心。"
msgstr "Biyi once preserved Penglai's writing, calendars, and knowledge, and long regarded themselves as the most reliable interpreters of its native culture. Chinese-language administration, printing, maritime education, and imported technology rapidly eroded that status. They came to understand unemployment, broken traditions, and dwindling opportunities as outsiders taking what had once been theirs. Biyi increasingly seek to restore their language, intellectual traditions, and even political institutions to the center of Penglai."

msgid "比翼把知识地位的流失首先体验为职位与发展机会减少。就业不足会成为两半共同的焦虑；阴月又担心公共机构缺少税课，阳月则担心新的知识产业缺少投资。"
msgstr "Biyi experience the loss of intellectual status first as fewer jobs and fewer opportunities. Weak employment troubles both halves; in Yin months they also worry that public institutions lack tax revenue, while in Yang months they fear new knowledge industries lack investment."

msgid "驻岁曾替各族见证旧约、调停争端，因为寿命漫长，人们相信他们比谁都懂得等待。但这个几乎无法繁衍的种族，也曾因害怕落后而追逐更快的生产、更早的收益和更激烈的竞争，最终只剩下玩家与助手。如今他们对“危机将至”格外迟疑，并非天生超然，而是因为整个种族已经为这种恐惧付过一次代价。"
msgstr "Zhushui once witnessed old covenants and mediated disputes among every people. Because they lived so long, others believed no one understood patience better. Yet this almost infertile people once feared being left behind and chased faster production, earlier returns, and harsher competition until only the player and the assistant remained. Their hesitation toward every warning that a crisis is coming is not innate detachment; their entire people have already paid for that fear once."

msgid "驻岁并不追求增长，只把五项公共指标的零点视为共同底线。任何一项跌入负值，都意味着玩家正在把一次极端构筑维持得太久，连助手也开始怀疑是否还值得继续。"
msgstr "Zhushui do not seek growth. They treat zero across the five public metrics as a common floor. If any metric falls below zero, the player has kept an extreme arrangement in place too long, and even the assistant begins to wonder whether it is worth continuing."

# Interest-group descriptions
msgid "人类医药传入蓬莱后，人们很快发现，同一味药、同一种疗法落在不同种族身上，往往会产生截然不同的结果。来自世界各地的医生因此汇聚蓬莱，与长期保存异族知识的比翼学者共同建立了医学会。如今，从药物、诊疗到异族病例与医学知识，几乎都掌握在这个学术共同体手中——它既是研究蓬莱生命的最高学府，也是蓬莱医药业事实上的垄断者。"
msgstr "After human medicine reached Penglai, it soon became clear that the same drug or treatment could produce radically different results in different peoples. Physicians from around the world gathered on the island and, together with Biyi scholars who had long preserved knowledge of other peoples, founded the Medical Society. Today this academic community controls nearly everything from medicines and clinical practice to case records and medical knowledge: it is both Penglai's foremost institution for the study of life and the de facto monopoly of its medical industry."

msgid "以遍布全岛的古老听弦塔为名，掌握未然取能、未然胆制造、能源配给与相关技术开发的组织。没有人知道那些古塔从何而来，但今日蓬莱的工场、偃偶、航船和远距通信都离不开从塔中取出的未然。于是，谁掌握听弦塔，谁便握住了这个工业时代最接近血液的东西。"
msgstr "Named for the ancient Tingxian Towers scattered across the island, this organization controls Weiran extraction, Weiran-cell manufacture, energy distribution, and related technology. No one knows where the old towers came from, but Penglai's factories, Yan'ou, ships, and long-distance communications all depend on the Weiran drawn from them. Whoever controls the towers therefore holds the closest thing this industrial age has to blood."

msgid "在人类带来白银与铜钱以前，各族便已经以未然互相借贷；那些跨越漫长年月仍能兑现的契约，大多需要驻岁出面作保。围绕这种信用逐渐形成的岁契基金延续至今，成为蓬莱几乎唯一真正意义上的银行，经营存贷、收债以及由此衍生的金融业务。岛上无数本土商号、工场与小业主都是它的债务人，因此岁契基金代表的从来不只是一家银行，而是整个依靠借来的钱维持运转的本土商业社会。"
msgstr "Long before humans brought silver and copper coin, Penglai's peoples lent Weiran to one another. Contracts expected to remain valid across great spans of time usually required a Zhushui guarantor. The credit institutions that grew around those guarantees became the Long Covenant Fund, now almost the island's only bank in the modern sense, handling deposits, loans, debt collection, and related finance. Countless local firms, workshops, and small proprietors owe it money, so the Fund represents more than a bank: it represents an entire local commercial society kept running on borrowed funds."

msgid "从永乐港的贡船、码头、仓栈与远洋船队中成长起来的庞大商办机构，几乎垄断了蓬莱与外界之间的航运。漫长海路总与沉船、失货和延期相伴，永乐局于是又替船货承担风险，并由此发展出保险、信托、股份等尚显稚嫩的金融业务。今天，人们托它把货送往世界，也把一部分风险、财富与未来交到它手中。"
msgstr "A vast commercial institution grown from Yongle Harbor's tribute ships, docks, warehouses, and ocean fleets, the Yongle Bureau nearly monopolizes shipping between Penglai and the outside world. Long voyages bring wrecks, lost cargo, and delays, so the Bureau began underwriting those risks and developed still-young businesses in insurance, trusts, and shares. Today people entrust it not only with carrying goods into the world, but with part of their risk, wealth, and future."

msgid "掌管行身制造、机谱、机件规格、身契登记与维修网络的庞大公所。每一具新偃偶的诞生、每一次大修乃至身体如何被买卖，都绕不开它制定的标准。它既把自己称作偃偶一族得以延续的保障，也让偃偶从出生起便活在它的账册、工场与契约之中。"
msgstr "A vast guild controlling body manufacture, machine schematics, component standards, body-contract registration, and maintenance networks. Every new Yan'ou, every major repair, and even the terms on which bodies are bought and sold must pass through standards it sets. It calls itself the guarantee of Yan'ou continuity, while ensuring that every Yan'ou lives from birth inside its ledgers, workshops, and contracts."

msgid "蓬莱的报纸、告示、统计与公共消息几乎都从这里排印出去。它不仅经营新闻，也决定什么值得成为新闻、哪些数字应当登上头版，以及全岛在同一个早晨讨论什么。《听弦》正是其发行的严肃政治经济报纸；许多蓬莱人以为自己只是从报纸上了解世界，却很少意识到，他们了解世界的方式本身也掌握在报馆手中。"
msgstr "Nearly all of Penglai's newspapers, notices, statistics, and public bulletins are typeset here. The Press does not merely sell news; it decides what deserves to become news, which figures belong on the front page, and what the whole island will discuss on the same morning. Tingxian, its serious political and economic paper, is only the clearest example. Many Penglai residents think they merely learn about the world from newspapers, rarely noticing that the very form of that knowledge is also in the Press's hands."

